{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The sum of all possible values of n is 16592.","counterconditions":["A prime p exists such that p^20 is in the range [2006, 12005)","Other prime pairs (p, q) for p^6 * q^2 were missed within the range"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Let m = n + 2005. For m to have exactly 21 factors, its prime factorization must be of the form p^20 or p^6 * q^2. Given 1 <= n < 10000, we have 2006 <= m < 12005. For p^20, 2^20 is far too large. For p^6 * q^2: if p=2, m=64q^2, then 31.34 <= q^2 < 187.57, so q in {7, 11, 13} giving m in {3136, 7744, 10816}. If p=3, m=729q^2, then 2.75 <= q^2 < 16.45, so q=2 giving m=2916. If p=5, m=15625q^2, which is too large. The possible values for m are {2916, 3136, 7744, 10816}, corresponding to n = {911, 1131, 5739, 8811}. The sum is 911 + 1131 + 5739 + 8811 = 16592.","optional_refs":["c66558e3efe468e1993596ce4e2319b40d41daaf548d369410d8014a23cf6d3a"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":1.0},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The sum of all possible values of n is 17523.","counterconditions":["The calculation in SRC_006 is verified as the only exhaustive search of the prime space"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"This candidate explores the possibility that a different set of prime pairs or a different interpretation of the range was used, possibly including m=p^2*q^6 as a distinct set of values, though algebraically it is symmetric to p^6*q^2.","optional_refs":["a0fd1260f5f33460dcdf2b32dd40ab41c01653abcb81d82f0eb96df7cf72f5b5"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The sum of all possible values of n is 2448.","counterconditions":["The requirement of exactly 21 factors necessitates p^6 * q^2 or p^20"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"This considers a scenario where the constraint on n is interpreted differently or the prime factorization requirement was severely misunderstood (e.g